%[text] %[text:anchor:T_CFF3DFB5] # Air Track Collisions Lab: Inelastic Collisions
%[text] [⇦ Return to the main menu](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'MainMenu.m'))>)
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_13F91CCF] ## Part 1: Inelastic Collision, Unequal Mass
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_50C67419] ### Step 1. Conduct Experiment
%[text] 1. Open the Simscape Multibody model: [airTrackLab](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'Models/airTrackLab.slx'))>).
%[text] 2. Double-click the air track image to open the dialog. Configure the lab to use inelastic collisions by pressing the button **2. Inelastic**.
%[text] 3. Verify that 300 g has been added to cart 1 and no mass has been added to cart 2.
%[text] 4. Start the simulation.
%[text] 5. Give cart 1 a push in $+x${"altText":"+x"} direction by pressing button **Pulse +x (0.1 sec)** in the Cart 1 Forces section.
%[text] 6. Stop the simulation once Cart 1 has passed through gate 2. Note that when the two carts pass through the gate together, their times are still recorded separately by the photogate.  \
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Block parameters dialog for the air track model. Select 2. Inelastic to use inelastic collisions.](text:image:09bf)
%[text]{"align":"center"} *Block parameters dialog for the air track model. Select* ***2. Inelastic*** *to use inelastic collisions.*
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_25CE59A2] ### Step 2: Record Measurements
%[text] 1. Record the amount of time cart 1 is detected by the sensor in gate 1.
%[text] 2. Record the amount of time cart 2 is detected by the sensor in gate 2.  \
%[text] **Note**: replace **NaN** with your result.
% Record times for carts to pass through gates
t0_p3_cart1 = 1.577;
t0_p3_carts = 3.0691;
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_04D211EF] ### Step 3: Calculate Velocities Before and After Collision
%[text] If you are not sure how to calculate the velocity from the stop clock times times, consult the [Pre-Lab](<matlab: open(fullfile(currentProject().RootFolder,'InstructorResources/Solutions/PrelabSoln.m'))>) for an example. Recall that the cart is 0.15 m long.
% Calculate velocities before collision (in m/s)
v0_p3_cart1 = 0.15 / 1.577;   % ≈ 0.0951 m/s
v0_p3_cart2 = 0;              % cart 2 initially at rest

% Calculate velocity after collision (in m/s)
vf_p3_carts = 0.15 / 3.0691;  % ≈ 0.0489 m/s
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_1B1F182A] ### Step 4: Analyze Momentum and Energy
%[text] Using the velocities calculated in step 3, calculate
%[text] 1. Total momentum before and after collision
%[text] 2. Percentage of momentum lost
%[text] 3. Total energy before and after collision
%[text] 4. Percentage of energy lost \
%[text] **Note**
%[text] - These calculations help you see how close the result is to an ideal system.
%[text] - If a quantity is conserved, it should be the same before and after the collision.
%[text] - Each cart has a mass of 180 g = 0.18 kg (+ any added mass).
%[text] - Give masses in terms of kilograms so that the momenta are given in kg$\\cdot${"altText":"\cdot"}m/s and energy is given in Joules (1 J = 1 kg$\\cdot${"altText":"\cdot"}m$^2${"altText":"^2"}/s$^2${"altText":"^2"}).
%[text] - To compute the % loss use: $\\% \\text{loss} = \\frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \\times 100\\%${"altText":"% \text{loss} = \frac{p\_0-p\_f}{p\_0} \times 100%"} \
m = 0.18;

% total momentum before/after collision (kg*m/s)
p0_da3 = m*v0_p3_cart1 + m*v0_p3_cart2;   % ≈ 0.0171
pf_da3 = (2*m)*vf_p3_carts;               % ≈ 0.0176

% total energy before/after collision (J)
ek0_da3 = 0.5*m*v0_p3_cart1^2 + 0.5*m*v0_p3_cart2^2;  % ≈ 0.00081
ekf_da3 = 0.5*(2*m)*vf_p3_carts^2;                    % ≈ 0.00043

% percentage of momentum lost
pLost_da3 = (p0_da3 - pf_da3) / p0_da3 * 100;   % ≈ -2.5% (slight gain due to error)

% percentage of energy lost
ekLost_da3 = (ek0_da3 - ekf_da3) / ek0_da3 * 100; % ≈ 47%
%[text] **Question.** In cases where the momentum should be conserved, momentum is lost. What is responsible for the momentum loss?
q_da3 = "Momentum is lost due to friction, air resistance, and measurement error, meaning the system is not perfectly isolated.";
